{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"AWS Security Hub CSPM launches AI Security Best Practices standard with 31 automated controls - AWS","context":"AWS Security Hub CSPM has introduced the AI Security Best Practices standard, which includes 31 automated security controls for detecting misalignments between security best practices and deployed AI resources. These controls cover critical security domains such as network isolation, encryption, VPC placement, KMS key usage, private container registry requirements, and authorization controls. The standard applies to various components of AI infrastructure, including Bedrock AgentCore runtimes, gateways, memory stores, custom browsers, SageMaker notebook instances, endpoints, models, monitoring jobs, and feature groups. The standard is available in all AWS Regions where Security Hub CSPM is available and can be accessed using the identifier \"standards/ai-security-best-practices/v/1.0.0\". Security teams can quickly identify and remediate misconfigurations using findings generated by each control, which is assigned a security category. The AI Security Best Practices standard is available at no cost for 30 days as part of the AWS Free Tier","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/06/aws-security-hub-cspm-ai-security/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"aws-security","skill_description":"Covers AWS security services and workflows — Security Hub V2 (OCSF) findings, connectors, aggregators, automation rules, and security posture summaries; Security Hub CSPM (V1/ASFF) controls and compliance standards; GuardDuty threat findings; Inspector vulnerability findings; Macie sensitive data findings; Detective investigation; and Security Lake configuration and data aggregation. Applicable when questions involve security posture, Exposure findings, CSPM failed controls, threat findings, vulnerability findings, sensitive data findings, automation rules, or cross-service security configuration across AWS environments. Procedures use standard AWS CLI syntax and work with or without the AWS MCP server.\n\nServices: guardduty, inspector2, securityhub, macie2, detective, securitylake\nTasks: review, summarize, configure\nPersona: security-engineer, cloud-architect\nWorkload: security\n*","skill_name":"aws-security"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Amazon Bedrock Guardrails announces general availability of cross-account safeguards - AWS","context":"Amazon Bedrock Guardrails has announced the general availability of cross-account safeguards, allowing centralized enforcement of safety controls across all AWS accounts within an organization. This feature offers configurable safeguards that block up to 88% of harmful multimodal content and filter hallucinated responses from foundation models. Central security teams and administrators can implement these controls automatically for all foundation model interactions in Amazon Bedrock, eliminating the need to manually configure guardrails for each account. Cross-account safeguards enable organization-wide enforcement from a single control point in the management account, with options for organization-level, account-level, and application-specific safeguards. This capability is available in all AWS commercial and GovCloud regions where Bedrock Guardrails is supported, and can be accessed through the AWS management console or APIs","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/04/bedrock-guardrails-cross-account-safeguards/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"amazon-bedrock","skill_description":"Builds generative AI applications on Amazon Bedrock. Covers model invocation (Converse API, InvokeModel), RAG with Knowledge Bases, Bedrock Agents, Guardrails, and AgentCore (including the Harness managed agent loop). Applies when invoking models, setting up Knowledge Bases, deploying to AgentCore, migrating/porting/converting a Bedrock Agent (including inline agents) to an AgentCore Harness, troubleshooting Bedrock errors (ThrottlingException, AccessDeniedException), or choosing Bedrock models (Claude, Llama, Nova, Titan). Also for KB migration to Managed, prompt caching, quota and throttling diagnosis, cost tracking, migrating between Claude model generations, chunking strategies, and Bedrock model selection. Covers AgentCore Payments (x402, microtransactions, Payment Manager, Connector, Instrument, Coinbase CDP, Stripe Privy, paid endpoints, agent payments). NOT for custom model training, Rekognition, or Comprehend. For SageMaker model discovery, customization, or endpoints, use aws-ai-ml.\n\nServices: bedrock, bedrock-runtime, bedrock-mantle, bedrock-agent, bedrock-agent-runtime, bedrock-agentcore-control, bedrock-agentcore\nTasks: deploy, debug, optimize, design, migrate\nPersona: developer, devops, architect\nWorkload: generative-ai, rag, agents\n*","skill_name":"amazon-bedrock"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"resilience-hub-multi-account","skill_description":"Configures AWS Resilience Hub v2 for multi-account resilience management across an AWS Organization. Covers the per-service cross-account permission model, cross-account IAM roles, and centralized assessment from a single account. Applies when the user wants to set up org-wide resilience or assess workloads that span multiple AWS accounts.\n\n\nServices: resiliencehub, organizations\nTasks: configure\nPersona: architect, devops\nWorkload: resilience\n*","skill_name":"resilience-hub-multi-account"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Search Security Hub and Config Bedrock controls"}

